.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-STOP-QUEUE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-stop-queue \- stop receiving from a queue, without stopping sends
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eqs stop-queue
.BR \-\-queue | \-q
.I ERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stops consumption of a queue while leaving production untouched.
.PP
Producers are unaffected: the queue goes on accepting everything sent to
it, and messages accumulate exactly as they would while no consumer
happened to be running.
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1)
is refused with
.I "HTTP 409"
until
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-start-queue (1)
lifts it.
.PP
Being a property of the queue rather than an access rule, it stops every
consumer at once rather than one identity at a time \- which is what
makes it usable for a maintenance window, where the point is that
.I nothing
takes work out, not that a particular caller may not.
.SH WHY IT REFUSES RATHER THAN RETURNING EMPTY
A stopped queue could just as easily answer a receive with an empty list,
and that would keep consumer logs quiet. It deliberately does not.
.PP
A queue that reads as empty is indistinguishable from a queue nobody is
writing to, and telling those two apart is exactly what whoever stopped
it needs to do. So expect consumers to log errors for as long as the
queue is stopped \- that noise is the signal that consumption is being
held, and its absence would be the bug.
.SH MESSAGES ALREADY IN FLIGHT
Left alone. A consumer that received a message before the stop is still
entitled to finish and delete it: deleting is not receiving, and
stopping a queue should not turn every lease consumers are holding into
a redelivery.
.PP
Their visibility timeouts run as normal, so a message whose consumer
does die comes back to the queue and then stays there \- nothing can
receive it \- until the queue is started again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fIERN\fR
ERN of the queue to stop. Required. Resolve a name to its ERN with
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue-ern (1).
.SH OUTPUT
The JSON response is printed to standard output:
.RS
.TP
.I ern
The queue that was stopped.
.TP
.I status
.I STOPPED
\- what is now recorded for it. A queue that may be received from reads
.IR AVAILABLE ,
which is also what every queue created before this existed reads as.
.TP
.I available
How many messages are receivable at this moment, which is what will have
accumulated by the time it is started again, plus whatever arrives
meanwhile.
.RE
.PP
Stopping a queue that is already stopped changes nothing, answers the
same way, and is not an error.
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-list-queues (1)
reports the flag for every queue.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The queue is recorded as stopped.
.TP
.B 1
The queue ERN is missing or names no queue, the caller is not authorized
for it, the request could not be sent, or the server rejected it.
Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Hold consumption while a broken handler is fixed, leaving producers
running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs stop-queue \e
    --queue ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:onix-queue
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what has piled up, then let consumers back in:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs get-message-count \e
    --queue ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:onix-queue

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs start-queue \e
    --queue ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:onix-queue
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect the messages without consuming them, which stays allowed while
the queue is stopped:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eqs list-messages \e
    --queue ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:queue:onix-queue
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-start-queue (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-list-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-queue-ern (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
